item.text:
**Reused Evidence**
None. No prior accepted item genuinely carries over to Chapter 4B.

**New Evidence**
- March 2022 electoral-law changes moved the playing field toward the incumbent and weakened contestability.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkey Freedom in the World 2023 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/turkey/freedom-world/2023","claim":"In March 2022, an AKP-backed electoral law lowered the parliamentary threshold from 10% to 7%, changed seat allocation among alliances, and exempted the president from rules limiting the use of state resources in campaigns.","locator":"Key Developments in 2022","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Freedom House gives a dated country-specific summary of an enacted law and its political effect; the claim is concrete and directly tied to the target year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 legislative changes, published in the next annual report cycle.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Erdogan because the AKP-led majority passed the law and the president benefited from the exemption, though the measure was enacted by parliament rather than by decree.","contrary_evidence":["Opposition parties argued the reform was designed to hinder a broad opposition alliance ahead of the 2023 elections."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.2","4B.8","4B.10"]}

- The October 2022 disinformation law expanded censorship pressure on media and online speech.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkey Freedom in the World 2023 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/turkey/freedom-world/2023","claim":"Parliament approved an October 2022 law that criminalized promoting false information on social media with prison terms of up to three years, and press-freedom advocates warned it could be used to silence independent journalists.","locator":"Key Developments in 2022 / D1","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Country report with a specific dated legal change and a direct description of its likely political use.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about a 2022 law and its immediate political effect.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly attributable to Erdogan because the AKP-led parliamentary majority enacted the law and it fit a broader government pattern of media control.","contrary_evidence":["The government said it was combating misinformation and protecting the public from false content."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}

- Politicized prosecutions of opposition figures and civil-society actors remained central in 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkey Freedom in the World 2023 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/turkey/freedom-world/2023","claim":"The report says Istanbul mayor Ekrem Imamoglu was convicted in December 2022, Canan Kaftancioglu's conviction was upheld in May, and the Kavala case showed continued disregard for ECHR rulings.","locator":"Key Developments in 2022 / F1","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The report names the actors, dates, and legal outcomes and links them to political motivation and judicial control.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 court actions and their political context.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Erdogan because the cases targeted his main opposition and civil-society critics within a judicial system the report describes as politically constrained.","contrary_evidence":["The government and courts maintained the cases were ordinary criminal proceedings."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.4","4B.5","4B.8","4B.10"]}

- HRW describes a broader authoritarian pattern: critics targeted, media controlled, judiciary constrained.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Turkey","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/turkey","claim":"HRW said Erdogan's government regularly targeted perceived critics and political opponents and exerted strong control over the media and judiciary.","locator":"Country chapter overview / Freedom of Expression, Association, and Assembly","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Major rights-monitoring annual report with direct country-level assessment and repeated corroborating examples.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers the 2022 target period in the 2023 annual cycle and summarizes the year-end posture.","ruler_attribution":"Strong attribution to Erdogan as the dominant executive and ruling-party figure; the report frames the system as government-directed rather than isolated misconduct.","contrary_evidence":["HRW notes that independent outlets and formal institutions still existed, but under heavy pressure."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.4","4B.5","4B.6","4B.10"]}

- Journalists were jailed at scale in 2022, especially Kurdish journalists.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Attacks on the Press in 2022","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-12","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/12/attacks-on-the-press-in-2022/","claim":"Turkey's jailed-journalist count rose from 18 in 2021 to 40 in 2022 after the arrests of 25 Kurdish journalists, whom lawyers said were charged on terrorism allegations tied to their work.","locator":"Turkey section","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"CPJ is the core specialist monitor for press repression and the claim is specific, numerical, and contemporaneous to the target year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 outcomes in the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Erdogan-era information environment and anti-terror enforcement machinery; the immediate arrests were made by security and judicial authorities, not Erdogan personally.","contrary_evidence":["Authorities framed the cases as anti-terrorism enforcement."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}

- The disinformation law created a clear chilling effect on online speech.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkey Dark day for online free expression as new disinformation law is passed","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-10-13","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/10/turkey-dark-day-for-online-free-expression-as-new-disinformation-law-is-passed/","claim":"Amnesty said the law tightened government control over social media and news websites and could send people to prison for up to three years for reposting content, creating a chilling effect.","locator":"opening paragraphs / Amnesty response","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Immediate contemporaneous rights analysis from a major NGO describing the law's likely effect and enforcement risk.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly tied to a 2022 statute and its immediate adoption.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the AKP-led government under Erdogan because the law was a state policy choice and part of a broader censorship pattern.","contrary_evidence":["The government presented the law as an anti-disinformation measure ahead of elections."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}

- The Council of Europe said the draft law interfered with free expression and lacked a pressing need.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkiye draft criminal provision on false or misleading information interferes with freedom of expression says Venice Commission","publisher":"Council of Europe","publication_date":"2022-10-10","url":"https://www.coe.int/en/web/portal/-/turkiye-draft-criminal-provision-on-false-or-misleading-information-interferes-with-freedom-of-expression-says-venice-commission?inheritRedirect=true&redirect=%2Fen%2Fweb%2Fcm%2Fnews%2F-%2Fasset_publisher%2FhwwluK1RCEJo%2Fcontent%2Fimpact-box-8-discrimination%2F16695","claim":"The Venice Commission said the draft criminal provision interfered with freedom of expression and that there was no pressing social need to introduce it.","locator":"press release body / opinion summary","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official Council of Europe opinion summarizing the legal defect in a draft law that was under active consideration in 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about a 2022 draft and the legal review that followed.","ruler_attribution":"Strong attribution to the Erdogan government because the draft came from the Turkish state and the warning was about its use against speech.","contrary_evidence":["Turkish authorities argued the draft was needed to address misinformation and protect election integrity."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9"]}

- Protest handling and activist arrests showed ongoing pressure on dissent and women's organizing.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Turkey","publisher":"U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089143.html","claim":"The report says police used riot shields, tear gas, pepper spray, and detentions against demonstrators, and in Diyarbakir authorities detained 24 women's rights activists, 11 of whom were later arrested.","locator":"report narrative on protests and Diyarbakir arrests","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual U.S. government report with concrete incident reporting and a specific target-year pattern of protest suppression and activist arrests.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Targets 2022 incidents even though the report was published in the next calendar year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable in part to Erdogan because the security and judicial organs operated under his government; the immediate acts were by police and prosecutors.","contrary_evidence":["Authorities treated the actions as policing and anti-terror enforcement rather than repression."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.7","4B.8","4B.9"]}

- Kavala's prosecution remained a flagship example of judicial capture and refusal to respect independent constraints.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkey Life Sentence for Rights Defender Osman Kavala","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-04-26","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/04/26/turkey-life-sentence-rights-defender-osman-kavala","claim":"HRW said Kavala and seven co-defendants were sentenced in a sham trial and that the verdict was proof Turkey's courts were operating under instructions from the Erdogan presidency.","locator":"body of news release","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct rights-organization statement on a major political case with explicit attribution and contemporaneous legal context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 case and ruling.","ruler_attribution":"Strong attribution to Erdogan because the case targeted one of his most prominent civil-society critics and the organization says the judiciary was acting under presidential direction.","contrary_evidence":["The Turkish court and government said the case was based on serious criminal allegations."],"lenses":["4B.4","4B.5","4B.8","4B.10"]}

- The EU and ECtHR said the Kavala detention was politically motivated.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkey Statement by the High Representative on the conviction of Mr. Osman Kavala","publisher":"European External Action Service","publication_date":"2022-04-26","url":"https://www.eeas.europa.eu/eeas/turkey-statement-high-representative-conviction-mr-osman-kavala_en","claim":"The EU said the European Court of Human Rights had found Kavala's arrest and pretrial detention pursued an ulterior purpose to silence him and dissuade other human rights defenders.","locator":"statement body","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official EU statement summarizing a binding ECtHR ruling and the resulting rule-of-law concern.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruling and reaction.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly attributable to the Erdogan government because the EU statement concerns the state's handling of a politically sensitive defendant.","contrary_evidence":["Turkey had not implemented the ECHR ruling and maintained the prosecution was lawful."],"lenses":["4B.4","4B.8","4B.10"]}

- The Gülsen case is a useful example of selective punishment for unpopular speech and identity politics.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkish pop star's arrest over religious schools quip stirs fierce criticism","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-08-27","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/08/27/turkey-court-gulsen","claim":"Gulsen was jailed pending trial after a joke about religious schools, and critics said the case targeted her liberal views and support for LGBT+ rights.","locator":"article body / critics and background paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire reporting is contemporaneous and specific, but it remains secondary reporting rather than a legal record.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 incident.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Erdogan-era climate because the arrest was made by Turkish authorities and critics linked it to government-aligned social control, though the immediate decision came from prosecutors and a court.","contrary_evidence":["Authorities said the prosecution concerned incitement to hatred rather than her political views."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.7","4B.9"]}

- The legal environment did not protect SOGIESC equality in practice.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkiye Treatment of persons with diverse sexual orientation, gender identity and expression, and sex characteristics (SOGIESC) by society and state authorities, including state protection (2020-November 2022)","publisher":"Immigration and Refugee Board of Canada","publication_date":"2022-11-23","url":"https://irb-cisr.gc.ca/en/country-information/rir/Pages/index.aspx?doc=458733","claim":"Same-sex relations are legal, but Turkish law does not explicitly protect sexual orientation or gender identity, and equality protections do not reliably hold in practice.","locator":"Section 1.1 Legislation","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official country-information review compiled from multiple sources; useful for legal status and protection gaps, though it is a secondary synthesis.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2020-November 2022 and thus fits the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Erdogan-era state because the legal and protection environment described is the product of current government policy and enforcement choices.","contrary_evidence":["The constitution contains a general equality principle, and same-sex relations are not criminalized."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.7"]}

- Narrow favorable counterpoint: the government did not deport Uyghur Muslims in 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Report on International Religious Freedom: Turkey (Turkiye)","publisher":"U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://2021-2025.state.gov/reports/2022-report-on-international-religious-freedom/turkey/","claim":"The report says the government showed a willingness to protect Uyghur Muslims in the country and did not deport any Uyghurs during the year.","locator":"Executive Summary / Uyghur passage","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official annual report with a specific favorable finding, but it is narrower and more thematic than the core political-freedom sources.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Targets 2022, though published later in the annual cycle.","ruler_attribution":"This is a narrow favorable counterpoint to the broader rights record; it reflects a specific protection decision rather than a general democratic opening.","contrary_evidence":["The same report also describes restrictions affecting other minorities and religious communities."],"lenses":["4B.7","4B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I reviewed roughly 30 documents across Freedom House, HRW, CPJ, Amnesty, the Council of Europe/Venice Commission, U.S. State Department reports, EEAS, IRB Canada, Reuters/Euronews wires, and a few Turkish/English mirrors.
- I kept 11 distinct URLs and accepted 13 claim units because several sources covered multiple materially different 2022 facts.
- I rejected near-duplicates where the English and Turkish versions said the same thing, and Reuters wire stories syndicated through multiple outlets when one direct wire-based version was enough.
- I rejected broad 2023 election-runoff explainers and the 2023 OSCE election final report as out-of-period for the core 2022 question set, even though they were useful for background.
- I also rejected nuclear and international-conflict material from the earlier chapters because it does not answer Chapter 4B.
- I did not re-fetch local structured datasets such as V-Dem, Freedom House, RSF, WGI, BTI, or Polity because the local prior already supplied the relevant country-year context.

**Remaining Gaps**
- The biggest gap is direct, target-year evidence on how the March 2022 electoral-law changes affected real competition and candidate viability in practice.
- More primary material on appointments, RTUK, YSK, and lower-court staffing would sharpen attribution on 4B.4 and 4B.5.
- The dossier has only one narrow favorable counterpoint. More search would probably add corroboration of the same negative trajectory rather than change the overall picture, but it could improve balance for 4B.7 and 4B.10.
